	abstract = {Many plants accumulate organic osmolytes (com
patible solutes) such as proline, betaines, and sugar
 alcohols, when they are exposed to salinity stress.1
3Most intertidal macroalgae also utilize a com
plex set of physiological and biochemical changes
 for adaptation to fluctuating salinities.4 In addition,
 some macroalgae (Enteromorpha intestinalis and
 Ulva fasciata) accumulate proline in response to
 hypersaline conditions.5,6 A sterile mutant of
 U. pertusa grows on rocks in the upper and lower
 intertidal zones and as floating algae under natural
 conditions,7 suggesting that the alga is often
 exposed to rapid salinity changes and tolerant to
 the extensive ranges of salinity. The sterile
 U. pertusa is an appropriate species for investi
gation of algal salinity stress responses by artificial
 control of cultivation conditions, since the alga only
 develops vegetatively and can be maintained with
 ease under axenic laboratory conditions.7 However,
 adaptable ranges and biochemical stress responses
 to salinity changes in the sterile U. pertusa have not
 been studied in detail. The present study was
 aimed at clarifying some of the physiological and
 biochemical changes for acclimation to fluctuating
 salinities in the sterile U. pertusa.},
